361	 ## Rules the code keeps
362	 
363	-- **Purity.** `simulation`, `protocol` and `content` import no Phaser, Colyseus, DOM, timers, wall-clock time or `Math.random`; their tsconfig leaves out the DOM library, and `tests/unit/purity.test.ts` fails on a violation.
364	+- **Purity.** `simulation`, `protocol` and `content` import no Phaser, Colyseus, DOM, timers, wall-clock time or `Math.random`, and call no floating-point transcendental `Math` function (`sqrt`, `cbrt`, `hypot`, `pow`, `exp`, `log*`, trigonometric). Their tsconfig leaves out the DOM library, and `tests/unit/purity.test.ts` fails on a violation.
365	 - **Integers.** Every number in the simulation state is an integer. Positions are sub-units (256 per 16 px tile) on a 48 × 32 tile sea with the origin top-left. Diagonals are normalised with an integer square root, so `hashState` (64-bit FNV-1a over a canonical serialization) does not depend on the JavaScript engine.
366	-- **One clock.** The room steps the simulation 60 times a second with Colyseus `setFixedTimestep`, the accumulator-based successor of `setSimulationInterval`. World logic runs on frames divisible by 3 (20 Hz); the room then mirrors the plain state into its Colyseus Schema, which Colyseus patches to clients at 20 Hz.
367	-- **The server alone writes state.** Clients send only `input` messages: `{seq, frame, buttons, moveX, moveY}`. `InputGate` drops, and counts, messages over the rate cap (60 per second of match time) or over 128 bytes, malformed ones (including axes outside [-1, 1], which are rejected rather than clamped), ones with a sequence number that is not higher than the last one, and ones tagged more than 30 frames ahead of the server. A held input lapses to neutral after 15 frames without a fresh one. Unknown message types are dropped. Colyseus's own limits sit above these and do disconnect: WebSocket frames over 4 KiB and more than 240 messages a second.
368	-- **Joining.** `create` makes a room whose code is generated on the server. `join` finds a room by code through `filterBy(['code'])`. The room's static `onAuth` refuses a mismatched protocol version (426), an unknown code (404) or a full match (409, eight players) with a readable message that the client shows.
369	+- **One clock.** The room steps the simulation 60 times a second with Colyseus `setFixedTimestep`, the accumulator-based successor of `setSimulationInterval`. World logic runs on frames divisible by 3 (20 Hz); the room then mirrors the plain state into its Colyseus Schema, which Colyseus patches to clients at 20 Hz. An integration test measures both rates on a running room.
370	+- **The server alone writes state.** Clients send only `input` messages: `{seq, frame, buttons, moveX, moveY}`. `InputGate` drops, and counts, messages over the rate cap (60 per second of match time) or over 128 bytes, malformed ones (including axes outside [-1, 1], which are rejected rather than clamped), ones with a sequence number that is not higher than the last one, and ones tagged more than 30 frames ahead of the server. A held input lapses to neutral after 15 frames without a fresh one. The frame tag is only checked against that 30-frame window: an accepted input applies from the server's next step, whatever frame it names. That is sound only while clients do not predict. The combat spike must apply inputs at their tagged frame when it adds prediction and reconciliation (§15). Unknown message types are dropped. Colyseus's own limits sit above these and do disconnect: WebSocket frames over 4 KiB and more than 240 messages a second.
371	+- **Joining.** Colyseus serves `create`, `join`, `joinOrCreate` and `joinById` over HTTP to any caller and reserves a seat before any room-level check runs, so a private match is guarded in three layers:
372	+  - The static `onAuth` (`authorizeEntry`) runs before any seat is reserved. It refuses a mismatched protocol version (426). It also refuses a request that carries neither a `code` nor a `createToken`, or both, or a malformed token (400). A request with a code is refused when the code is unknown (404, a malformed code included) or the match is full (409, eight players).
373	+  - `create` carries a one-time `createToken` (128 random bits from the platform's crypto API: `crypto.getRandomValues` in the browser, `node:crypto` in tests). The room is registered with `filterBy(['code', 'createToken'])`. Colyseus copies filter fields from the create options into the listing's metadata, so `onCreate` replaces the metadata with `{code, custom}` before the listing is stored. No listing ever holds a token, so a `join` carrying one finds nothing and a `joinOrCreate` carrying one can only create. `onCreate` refuses to make a match for a request without a token.
374	+  - The room's `onJoin` is the backstop, and the only check a `joinById` meets after `onAuth`. It admits one seat carrying the creator's token, then only seats whose `code` equals the match's code (404 for another code, 403 otherwise). Its refusal frees the seat at once.
375	+
376	+  Refusal messages stay under 100 bytes, because a refusal from the room travels over the WebSocket. The client shows the server's messages. Colyseus's generic "no rooms found" (the match filled up or closed between the check and the seat, as when two players race for the last seat) is worded "That match is full or no longer exists". What remains: a caller who already knows a room id (a current or former member) can send `joinById` requests without connecting and hold seats until each reservation expires (Colyseus's seat reservation timeout, 15 s by default).
377	 - **Rendering.** The client keeps a snapshot buffer per ship and draws 100 ms in the past, interpolating between patches. The HUD lists the latest decoded server positions without interpolation. Prediction and reconciliation are left to the combat spike.
